From a security perspective, it would be easier to list
the problems that *doesn't* cause than the ones it does.
The fact that operating systems even allow it is an
unfortunate artifact of the fact that proper security has
been largely an afterthought in system design to date.
95% of all malware would cease to exist if no portion
of the filesystem could be both user-writable (for
anyone other than root) and also executable.

